@node Finding Conflicts
@section Finding Conflicts

jj labels a conflicted revision @samp{conflict} in the log.  The optional
@ref{Log Buffer, , Conflicts section} lists the working copy's conflicted files.

There are three ways to resolve a conflict:

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Way
@tab Start with
@tab Suits
@item Conflict workbench
@tab @code{? M}
@tab Several files@comma{} any revision
@item Ediff
@tab @code{E m}
@tab One file@comma{} at most two sides
@item Conflict markers
@tab @code{E M}@comma{} or open the file
@tab Editing the markers by hand
@end multitable

@node Conflict Workbench
@section Conflict Workbench

@table @asis
@item @kbd{? M} (@code{majutsu-resolve})
@kindex ? M
@findex majutsu-resolve
Open the workbench for the revision at point@comma{}
  or @samp{@@}.  With a prefix argument@comma{} choose a revision.
@end table

The workbench lists the conflicted files of one commit as a directory tree.
It works on any revision@comma{} not only the working copy.  You edit private
drafts of the files and apply them when you are satisfied; until then jj is
untouched.

A file unfolds into its conflicts@comma{} and a conflict into jj's terms: a
snapshot of one side@comma{} or a change with its @emph{before} and @emph{after}.  @code{TAB}
shows the text of a term.  Side numbers match those of the keep-side
commands in @ref{Conflict Markers, , conflict-marker buffers}.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{@key{RET}} (@code{majutsu-resolve-visit-file})
@kindex RET
@findex majutsu-resolve-visit-file
Open the file's draft@comma{} at the
  conflict or term at point.  The draft uses the file's usual major mode
  with @samp{majutsu-conflict-mode}.
@item @kbd{C-c C-c} (@code{majutsu-resolve-apply})
@kindex C-c C-c
@findex majutsu-resolve-apply
Apply the drafts of the file or
  directory at point in one jj operation.  With a prefix argument@comma{} apply all
  drafts.
@item @kbd{C-c C-k} (@code{majutsu-resolve-discard})
@kindex C-c C-k
@findex majutsu-resolve-discard
Discard the drafts of the file or
  directory at point@comma{} after confirmation.  With a prefix argument@comma{} discard
  all.  This does not undo anything already applied.
@item @kbd{C-c C-n} (@code{majutsu-resolve-next-file})
@kindex C-c C-n
@findex majutsu-resolve-next-file
Visit the next file that is
  still conflicted.
@item @kbd{?} (@code{majutsu-resolve-dispatch})
@kindex ?
@findex majutsu-resolve-dispatch
Show these actions as a transient: @code{a}
  applies and @code{k} discards at point@comma{} @code{A} and @code{K} act on all drafts@comma{} @code{g}
  refreshes@comma{} and @code{m} opens the Majutsu dispatcher.
@end table

In a draft@comma{} the same keys apply to that file@comma{} and @code{C-c ?} opens the transient.
@code{C-x C-s} saves the draft only.  @code{C-c C-o} returns to the workbench.

Drafts live as long as the workbench buffer.  Refreshing keeps them;
killing the workbench asks about pending drafts and then removes them.

After applying@comma{} Majutsu checks the new revision@comma{} the file contents@comma{} and the
remaining conflicts.  If it cannot verify the result@comma{} it keeps the drafts and
stops writing: look at what jj did@comma{} save any draft text you want@comma{} then kill
the workbench and open the revision again.

The workbench edits existing UTF-8 text files with conflict markers.  It
does not handle binary files@comma{} deletions@comma{} renames@comma{} or taking a whole file
from one side; use Ediff or edit the file for those.

@node Resolving with Ediff
@section Resolving with Ediff

@code{E m} (@code{majutsu-ediff-resolve}) runs @samp{jj resolve} with a three-way Ediff
session.  On a revision in the log it offers that revision's conflicted
files@comma{} otherwise the working copy's; candidates show the number of sides.

Quit Ediff to finish.  Majutsu then asks whether to save the merge;
answering no@comma{} or quitting without edits@comma{} leaves the conflict as it was.
Regions you left unresolved are written back with conflict markers@comma{} so jj
still reports them.

A conflict with more than two sides cannot be shown in Ediff; Majutsu opens
it with @samp{jj diffedit} instead.

@node Conflict Markers
@section Conflict Markers

@samp{majutsu-conflict-mode} is a minor mode for files that contain jj's conflict
markers.  It highlights the sides and has commands to keep one of them.

@code{E M} (@code{majutsu-ediff-resolve-with-conflict}) opens a conflicted file with
the mode enabled@comma{} at the first conflict.  For a revision other than the
working copy@comma{} it opens an editable blob@comma{} and saving it resolves the conflict
in that revision.  Workbench drafts use the mode too.

@deffn Command majutsu-conflict-ensure-mode
Enable the mode in a buffer with
  conflict markers.  A buffer with only Git-style markers (@samp{|||||||} /
  @samp{======}) gets @samp{smerge-mode} instead.
@end deffn

All of jj's marker styles are recognized@comma{} including long markers and
conflicts whose sides lack a final newline.

