@node Highlighting and Context Help
@subsection Highlighting and Context Help

Conflict regions are highlighted like smerge-mode@comma{} generalized to
n-sided conflicts:
@itemize
@item
Each side snapshot gets a distinct background face@comma{} cycling through
@code{majutsu-conflict-side-faces} (@samp{majutsu-conflict-side-1-face} inherits
@samp{smerge-upper}@comma{} @samp{side-2} inherits @samp{smerge-lower}).
@item
Base snapshots use @samp{majutsu-conflict-base-face} (inherits @samp{smerge-base}).
@item
Git-style (diff3) regions in mixed buffers (for example produced by
@code{majutsu-conflict-restyle}) are colored the same way. Buffers that contain
only git-style conflicts are still handed to @samp{smerge-mode} by
@code{majutsu-conflict-ensure-mode}; the two modes stay mutually exclusive.
@item
Eldoc describes the term at point ("jj conflict side 2/3: @dots{}").
@item
@samp{mouse-3} on a conflict pops up a resolution menu listing every side and
base with its revision label. With @samp{context-menu-mode} enabled@comma{} the entries
join the standard context menu instead of replacing it.
@item User Option: majutsu-conflict-auto-leave
When non-nil (the default)@comma{}
resolving the last conflict in the buffer turns off
@samp{majutsu-conflict-mode}; undo re-enables it.
@end itemize

