@node Understanding Bookmarks
@section Understanding Bookmarks

In Jujutsu@comma{} bookmarks are named references similar to Git branches. A local
bookmark normally targets one revision and may explicitly track a remote
bookmark; concurrent moves can also leave a bookmark with conflicted targets.

@node Bookmark List
@section Bookmark List

The bookmark list shows the local bookmarks@comma{} each with the state of its
remotes on its own line.

@example
main  ≡ origin            kzvx 1f2c first line of the description
topic ↑2 origin@comma{} ○ fork   qpwm 8a01 first line of the description
@end example

Unfold a bookmark for its remote bookmarks and@comma{} when its target is
conflicted@comma{} the targets jj removed (@samp{-}) and added (@samp{+}).  Git's special @samp{@@git} reference
appears under its bookmark only when it differs from the local target.
Bookmarks that exist on a remote only are in the @ref{Remote List, , remote list}.

@itemize
@item
@samp{≡} means the tracked reference is synchronized with the local bookmark.
@item
@samp{○} means the reference is not tracked.
@item
@samp{↑N} counts commits only in the local bookmark; @samp{↓N} counts commits only
in the remote reference.  Zero counts are omitted.  A lower bound is
shown as @samp{↑≥N} or @samp{↓≥N} rather than an exact count.
@item
@samp{×} means the local bookmark has been deleted while the tracked remote
reference remains.  The deleted local bookmark stays visible.
@end itemize

Synchronized and untracked states use a muted color; local-only and
remote-only counts use distinct colors.  Hover over a row for the symbol
legend.  Folding is retained on refresh.

@code{RET} on a bookmark shows the diff of its revision@comma{} and @code{k} (@code{x} in Evil) deletes the
bookmark.  @code{majutsu-bookmark-list} moves to the local bookmark of the
bookmark at point@comma{} so from a remote bookmark in the remote list it leads to
its local row.  @code{majutsu-bookmark-list-columns} customizes the rows; it has
the format of the @ref{Log Columns, , log columns}.

@node Remote List
@section Remote List

@code{majutsu-git-remote-list} shows the Git remotes with their fetch URL@comma{} a
distinct push URL@comma{} and how many of their bookmarks are tracked.

@example
origin  git@@example.org:me/repo.git  2 tracked
fork  git@@example.org:you/repo.git  1 tracked@comma{} 12 untracked
@end example

Unfold a remote for the bookmarks it holds@comma{} tracked or not@comma{} with the same
state symbols as the bookmark list.  This is where a bookmark that exists on
a remote only is found and tracked.  The bookmarks are rendered when the
remote is first unfolded.

On a remote@comma{} @code{f} fetches from it@comma{} @code{r} opens the Remotes transient@comma{} and @code{k} (@code{x}
in Evil) removes it.

The command moves to the remote at point: from a remote bookmark in the
bookmark list@comma{} it leads to the remote that holds it.

@node Projects in the jjosh Build
@section Projects in the jjosh Build

jjosh configures remotes per scope: the repository itself or one registered
project.  Both lists therefore have one section per scope: @samp{Repository}@comma{}
then each project under a heading with its name and directory.  The bookmark
list shows the scopes that have local bookmarks; the remote list shows every
project@comma{} and the repository when it has remotes of its own.

@example
parser  packages/parser  1 unsynced
  main  ≈ origin            kzvx 1f2c first line of the description
  topic ↑2 origin           qpwm 8a01 first line of the description
@end example

@example
parser  packages/parser
  origin  git@@example.org:me/parser.git  2 tracked
vendor  third_party/vendor
  origin  https://example.org/vendor.git  filter :/src  37 untracked
@end example

Bookmarks appear under their project without the @samp{#label} suffix; commands
still receive the full name.  Remotes act through their qualified name@comma{} such
as @samp{origin#parser}.

In a repository of many projects@comma{} a tracked remote reference usually names
another commit than the local bookmark merely because unrelated history was
rewritten.  @samp{≈} marks a project bookmark whose project directory is
identical on both sides; commit distances are shown only when the content
differs.  A push can still update the remote@comma{} because the history differs.
A project heading counts the bookmarks whose content differs as @samp{N unsynced}.

@code{majutsu-git-fetch-project} fetches the project at point from its default
remotes@comma{} and @code{majutsu-project-at-point} returns its name for your own
commands.  Neither has a default key binding.

