@node Understanding Workspaces
@section Understanding Workspaces

A workspace is a working copy with its own @samp{@@}; several workspaces can
share one repository@comma{} much like Git worktrees.  The log lists them below the
graph@comma{} and @code{RET} on one visits it.

@node Workspace Transient
@section Workspace Transient

@table @asis
@item @kbd{Z / % (Vanilla)} (@code{majutsu-workspace})
@kindex Z / % (Vanilla)
@findex majutsu-workspace
Open the workspace transient.
@item @kbd{* (Evil)} (@code{majutsu-workspace})
@kindex * (Evil)
@findex majutsu-workspace
Open the workspace transient.
@end table

Actions:
@table @asis
@item @kbd{l} (@code{majutsu-workspace-list})
@kindex l
@findex majutsu-workspace-list
List all workspaces.
@item @kbd{v} (@code{majutsu-workspace-visit})
@kindex v
@findex majutsu-workspace-visit
Visit a workspace by changing
  @samp{default-directory} and refreshing Majutsu buffers.
@item @kbd{a} (@code{majutsu-workspace-add})
@kindex a
@findex majutsu-workspace-add
Add a new workspace.
@item @kbd{f} (@code{majutsu-workspace-forget})
@kindex f
@findex majutsu-workspace-forget
Forget a workspace with
  @samp{jj workspace forget}. Its directory stays on disk@comma{} and changes jj has not
  snapshotted stay only there.
@item @kbd{k} (@code{majutsu-workspace-remove})
@kindex k
@findex majutsu-workspace-remove
Remove a workspace with
  @samp{jj workspace remove}@comma{} which deletes its directory. jj first snapshots the
  working copy into a commit@comma{} so tracked changes stay in the repository;
  untracked and ignored files are deleted. The workspace at point is the
  default@comma{} and the workspace that holds the repository cannot be removed.
@item @kbd{u} (@code{majutsu-workspace-update-stale})
@kindex u
@findex majutsu-workspace-update-stale
Update a stale workspace.
@item @kbd{n} (@code{majutsu-workspace-rename})
@kindex n
