@node Evil
@section Evil

Majutsu detects Evil and installs its own Normal-state bindings; this
manual lists them next to the Vanilla ones.

@defopt majutsu-evil-enable-integration
Set to @code{nil} to disable
  automatic Evil bindings.
@end defopt

@defopt majutsu-evil-initial-state
The Evil state to start in (default:
  @samp{normal}).
@end defopt

The dispatcher shows the Evil keys when it is opened from Normal@comma{} Visual@comma{}
or Motion state@comma{} and the Vanilla keys otherwise.  Evil's @code{v} stays Visual
state@comma{} so the evolution log is @code{? v}.

@node Embark
@section Embark

With Embark loaded@comma{} @samp{embark-act} works on bookmarks@comma{} revisions@comma{} and remotes
in Majutsu buffers and on bookmark and workspace completion candidates.
Majutsu binds no key for it; use your Embark key.

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Target
@tab Actions
@item Revision
@tab @code{RET} shows its diff@comma{} @code{e} edits it
@item Local bookmark
@tab the same@comma{} and @code{s} set@comma{} @code{m} / @code{M} move@comma{} @code{p} advance@comma{} @code{r} rename@comma{} @code{d} delete@comma{} @code{f} forget
@item Remote bookmark
@tab the same as a revision@comma{} and @code{t} track or @code{u} untrack
@item Remote
@tab @code{f} fetch@comma{} @code{r} rename@comma{} @code{d} remove
@item Workspace
@tab @code{RET} / @code{v} visit@comma{} @code{d} open in Dired@comma{} @code{W} copy the root
@end multitable

The default action shows a diff; editing the working copy is always the
explicit @code{e}.  A command such as Move still asks for its other arguments.
A target of a conflicted bookmark acts as its own revision@comma{} not as the
bookmark.  In the jjosh build@comma{} @code{f} on a remote or @samp{@@git} bookmark forgets it
until the next fetch.

@node Forge
@section Forge

With @uref{https://github.com/magit/forge, Forge} installed:

@lisp
(require 'majutsu-forge)
(majutsu-forge-mode 1)
@end lisp

The log then shows Forge's pull requests@comma{} issues@comma{} and discussions@comma{} and
@code{N} and @code{'} open @code{forge-dispatch}.  This is meant for colocated
repositories@comma{} where Forge can see a Git repository and its remotes; the
repository must be tracked in Forge's database.

@defopt majutsu-forge-add-default-sections
Whether to add the
  topic sections to the log.
@end defopt

@defopt majutsu-forge-add-default-bindings
Whether to bind @code{N} and
  @code{'}.
@end defopt

@defopt majutsu-forge-expand-sections
Whether the topic sections
  start unfolded.
@end defopt

@defopt majutsu-forge-suppress-pullreq-commits
Whether to leave out
  the Git commit list Forge shows inside a pull request.
@end defopt

@defopt majutsu-forge-show-section-errors
Whether to report errors
  from Forge's sections in the echo area.
@end defopt

@node Org Links
@section Org Links

Majutsu defines two Org link types@comma{} like Orgit does for Magit:

@example
majutsu:/path/to/repo/            opens the repository's log
majutsu-rev:/path/to/repo/::REV   opens the diff of the revision
@end example

@code{org-store-link} in a Majutsu buffer stores a link to the revision at point@comma{}
or to the repository; with several revisions in the region it stores one
link for each.  @code{org-insert-link} inserts them@comma{} and completes either type
by asking for a repository and a revision.  Nothing needs to be enabled.

@node Bug References
@section Bug References

Log and description buffers turn on @code{bug-reference-mode}@comma{} so issue
references such as @samp{#123} are buttons.  Majutsu derives the issue tracker
from the repository's Git remotes.

@defopt majutsu-bug-reference-remote-names
The remotes to try@comma{} in
  order (default: @samp{upstream}@comma{} then @samp{origin}).
@end defopt

In the @ref{jjosh Build, , jjosh build} each project has remotes of its own@comma{} so a reference
means the tracker of the project that its revision touches: @samp{#123} in a
revision of one project and in a revision of another lead to different
trackers.  Of nested projects the innermost counts@comma{} and a merge that
changes nothing stands for what its parents touch.  A reference that could
mean several trackers@comma{} or none@comma{} is not a button.  The log reads the
projects from its @code{projects} metadata column; keep it when you replace
@code{majutsu-log-commit-columns}.

Remove @code{bug-reference-mode} from @code{majutsu-log-mode-hook} and
@code{majutsu-jjdescription-setup-hook} to turn this off.

@node Remote Repositories
@section Remote Repositories

Majutsu works in a repository opened through TRAMP: jj runs on the remote
host@comma{} and blob buffers@comma{} Ediff@comma{} and description editing work there.

@defopt majutsu-remote-jj-executable
The jj executable on remote
  hosts (default: @samp{"jj"}).  @code{majutsu-jj-executable} applies to local
  repositories only.
@end defopt

Terminal programs@comma{} that is jj's built-in diff editor and Arrange@comma{} need
Ghostel and a POSIX remote.

@node jjosh Build
@section jjosh Build

Majutsu can be compiled for the jjosh fork of jj.  The variant is chosen
when the Lisp files are compiled@comma{} not at run time: set @code{MAJUTSU_BACKEND} to
@samp{jjosh} in the environment of the compiler.  Unset@comma{} or @samp{jj}@comma{} builds for
upstream Jujutsu.

@example
MAJUTSU_BACKEND=jjosh emacs -Q --batch \
  --eval '(package-initialize)' \
  -L . -l majutsu-jjosh.el -f batch-byte-compile majutsu*.el
@end example

Use the same setting for byte and native compilation@comma{} and compile into a
directory of its own.  Switching variants takes a full rebuild and a
restart of Emacs; libraries of the two must not be mixed.  When loading
from source@comma{} set the variable before the first Majutsu library loads.
@code{majutsu-build-backend} records the variant.

The jjosh build adds:

@itemize
@item
project sections in the @ref{Bookmarks, , bookmark and remote lists}@comma{}
@item
the touched-projects column in the log@comma{}
@item
@ref{Bug References, , bug references} resolved for each project@comma{}
@item
Forget for remote bookmarks@comma{}
@item
@code{majutsu-git-fetch-project}.
@end itemize

It needs a jjosh executable for @code{majutsu-jj-executable} and
@code{majutsu-remote-jj-executable}; nothing is probed.  The upstream build runs
with a jjosh executable too@comma{} and shows upstream's features only.

Code for one variant is wrapped in @code{majutsu-jjosh}@comma{} which expands to its
body in the jjosh build and to @code{nil} otherwise:

@lisp
(majutsu-jjosh
  (keymap-set majutsu-embark-git-bookmark-map "f"
              #'majutsu-bookmark-forget-ref))
@end lisp

