@node Overview
@section Overview

Majutsu includes a domain-specific language (DSL) for building Jujutsu
templates in Emacs Lisp. The main entry point is @samp{majutsu-tpl}@comma{} which compiles
a vector-based DSL form into a jj template string.

@node Why Use the DSL@?
@section Why Use the DSL@?

The DSL provides several advantages over writing raw template strings:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Early validation}: Constant vector forms with an explicit constant self type
are validated during macro expansion/byte-compilation. Dynamic forms and
forms that rely on the configurable default self type are validated when
Majutsu compiles them@comma{} before they are sent to jj.
@item
@strong{Automatic escaping}: String literals are properly escaped (quotes@comma{}
backslashes@comma{} control characters) without manual intervention.
@item
@strong{Elisp integration}: Embed Elisp expressions that are evaluated when Majutsu
compiles the template—during macro expansion for constant forms and at
runtime for dynamic forms—so configuration and context can shape the result.
@item
@strong{Composability}: Define reusable template functions with
@samp{majutsu-template-defun} that lower into the final template instead of
requiring jj-side aliases.
@item
@strong{Type awareness}: The DSL understands jj's type system@comma{} enabling self-type
context for cleaner keyword syntax (@samp{[:description]} instead of
@samp{[:method [:self] :description]}).
@item
@strong{Readability}: Vector-based syntax with keywords is more readable than deeply
nested string concatenation.
@end itemize

Example comparison:
@lisp
;; Raw string (error-prone@comma{} hard to read)
"if(self.root()@comma{} \"(root)\"@comma{} self.commit_id().short())"

;; DSL (validated@comma{} composable@comma{} readable)
(majutsu-tpl [:if [:root] "(root)" [:commit_id :short]] 'Commit)
@end lisp

@node Basic Syntax
@section Basic Syntax

@menu
* Vectors and Concatenation::
* String Literals::
* Raw Injection::
* Booleans and Numbers::
@end menu

@node Vectors and Concatenation
@subsection Vectors and Concatenation

Vectors without a leading keyword are implicitly concatenated:
@lisp
(majutsu-tpl ["A" "B"])           ; => "concat(\"A\"@comma{} \"B\")"
(majutsu-tpl [:concat "A" "B"])   ; => "concat(\"A\"@comma{} \"B\")"
@end lisp

Bare strings inside vectors are automatically treated as string literals (@samp{:str}).

@node String Literals
@subsection String Literals

Use @samp{:str} for explicit string literals with proper escaping:
@lisp
(majutsu-tpl [:str "Hello \"World\""])  ; => "Hello \"World\""
@end lisp

@node Raw Injection
@subsection Raw Injection

Use @samp{:raw} to inject template code directly without escaping:
@lisp
(majutsu-tpl [:raw "self.commit_id().short()"])  ; => "self.commit_id().short()"
@end lisp

An untyped raw snippet has the type @samp{Unknown}.  Annotate it to let method
chains be type-checked:
@lisp
[:raw "self" :Commit]
@end lisp

The callable position of @samp{:call} can be written as a quoted symbol@comma{} keyword@comma{} or
string name:
@lisp
(majutsu-tpl [:call 'coalesce [:str ""] [:str "X"]])  ; => "coalesce(\"\"@comma{} \"X\")"
@end lisp

@node Booleans and Numbers
@subsection Booleans and Numbers

Elisp @samp{t} and @samp{nil} map to @samp{true} and @samp{false}. Numbers pass through directly:
@lisp
(majutsu-tpl [:if t "yes" "no"])      ; => "if(true@comma{} \"yes\"@comma{} \"no\")"
(majutsu-tpl [:call 'pad_end 8 "x"])  ; => "pad_end(8@comma{} \"x\")"
@end lisp

@node Method Calls and Self
@section Method Calls and Self

@menu
* Explicit Method Chaining::
* Implicit Self Context::
* Explicit Receiver References::
* Binding Self in Helper Bodies::
@end menu

@node Explicit Method Chaining
@subsection Explicit Method Chaining

Use @samp{:method} to call methods on objects. Methods can be chained:
@lisp
(majutsu-tpl [:method [:raw "commit" :Commit] :commit_id])
; => "commit.commit_id()"

(majutsu-tpl [:method [:raw "commit" :Commit] :parents :len])
; => "commit.parents().len()"

(majutsu-tpl [:method [:raw "commit" :Commit] :diff "src"])
; => "commit.diff(\"src\")"
@end lisp

@node Implicit Self Context
@subsection Implicit Self Context

When a self type is given@comma{} or @code{majutsu-template-default-self-type} is set
(the default is @samp{Commit})@comma{} bare keywords are method calls on @samp{self}:
@lisp
(majutsu-tpl [:description] 'Commit)      ; => "self.description()"
(majutsu-tpl [:parents :len] 'Commit)     ; => "self.parents().len()"
@end lisp

@node Explicit Receiver References
@subsection Explicit Receiver References

Use @samp{[:self]} when you need the current implicit receiver as a value. When
nested lambdas or helper-local @samp{:bind-self} scopes introduce new receivers@comma{}
@samp{[:self N]} selects the outer binding @samp{N} levels up (@samp{[:self 0]} is the same
as @samp{[:self]}):
@lisp
(majutsu-tpl [:method [:self] :description] 'Commit)
; => "self.description()"

(majutsu-tpl [:method [:raw "self" :Commit]
                      :parents
                      :map
                      [:|p| [:method [:self 1] :description]]])
; => "self.parents().map(|p| self.description())"
@end lisp

@node Binding Self in Helper Bodies
@subsection Binding Self in Helper Bodies

Ordinary helpers can temporarily rebind the implicit @samp{self} inside their body
with @samp{:bind-self}:
@lisp
(majutsu-template-defun show-canonical-log-id ((object Commit :optional t))
  (:returns Template :bind-self object)
  [:canonical-log-id])
@end lisp

When the bound parameter is nil@comma{} the helper inherits the outer @samp{self} binding.

@node Operators
@section Operators

Arithmetic and logical operators are supported:
@lisp
(majutsu-tpl [:+ 1 2])              ; => "(1 + 2)"
(majutsu-tpl [:and [:> 3 1] [:<= 2 2]])  ; => "((3 > 1) && (2 <= 2))"
(majutsu-tpl [:not t])              ; => "(!true)"
(majutsu-tpl [:++ "L" "R"])         ; => "(\"L\" ++ \"R\")"
@end lisp

@node Conditionals and Composition
@section Conditionals and Composition

@lisp
(majutsu-tpl [:if [:root] "(root)" [:commit_id]])
; => "if(self.root()@comma{} \"(root)\"@comma{} self.commit_id())"

(majutsu-tpl [:separate " " [:label "a" "A"] [:label "b" "B"]])
; => "separate(\" \"@comma{} label(\"a\"@comma{} \"A\")@comma{} label(\"b\"@comma{} \"B\"))"
@end lisp

@node Elisp Embedding
@section Elisp Embedding

Elisp expressions are evaluated when the template is compiled.
@lisp
(let* ((tmp 1)
       (s1 `[:concat @comma{}(if (> 2 tmp) [:str "T"] [:str "F"]) [:str "!"]])
       (s2 [:concat (if (> 2 tmp) [:str "T"] [:str "F"]) [:str "!"]])
       (tmp 3))
  (concat (majutsu-tpl s1) (majutsu-tpl s2)))
; => "concat(\"T\"@comma{} \"!\")concat(\"F\"@comma{} \"!\")"
@end lisp

@node Anonymous Functions and Higher-Order Operations
@section Anonymous Functions and Higher-Order Operations

Anonymous functions are first-class template values:
@lisp
(majutsu-tpl [:lambda [c] [:description]])
; => "|c| c.description()"

(majutsu-tpl [:|c| [:description]])
; => "|c| c.description()"

(majutsu-tpl [:parents :map [:|c| [:description]]])
; => "self.parents().map(|c| c.description())"

(majutsu-tpl [:call [:|c| [:description]] [:raw "item" :Commit]])
; => "item.description()"

(majutsu-tpl [[:lambda [c] [:description]] [:raw "item" :Commit]])
; => "item.description()"
@end lisp

The shorthand @samp{[:|c| BODY]} is equivalent to @samp{[:lambda [c] BODY]}.

Inside a lambda@comma{} a bare keyword is a method call on the lambda's parameter:
in @samp{[:lambda [c] [:description]]}@comma{} @samp{[:description]} means
@samp{[:method 'c :description]}.  The parameter's type comes from where the
lambda is used@comma{} such as the elements of the list being mapped.  Parameters
are also ordinary variables: an inner lambda can name an outer parameter@comma{}
or reach an outer receiver with @samp{[:self N]}.

For example@comma{} the inner body below uses all three forms at once: @samp{[:description]}
for the inner receiver@comma{} @samp{[:self 1]} for the outer receiver@comma{} and explicit
@samp{[:method 'o ...]} for the outer lexical parameter:
@lisp
(majutsu-tpl
 [[:|o|
   [[:|i|
     [:if [:method 'o :root]
         [:description]
       [:method [:self 1] :description]]]
    [:raw "inner" :Commit]]]
  [:raw "outer" :Commit]])
; => "if(outer.root()@comma{} inner.description()@comma{} outer.description())"
@end lisp

List-oriented methods can be written in several equivalent styles:
@lisp
;; Binder form: collection@comma{} variable@comma{} body
(majutsu-tpl [:map [:raw "self.bookmarks()"] b [:raw "b.name()"]])
; => "self.bookmarks().map(|b| b.name())"

;; Direct jj-style method call with an anonymous lambda
(majutsu-tpl [:method [:raw "refs"] :map [:lambda [c] [:description]]])
; => "refs.map(|c| c.description())"

;; Dash-style explicit lambda
(majutsu-tpl [:-map [:lambda [c] [:description]] [:raw "refs"]])
; => "refs.map(|c| c.description())"

;; Dash-style anaphoric shorthand
(majutsu-tpl [:--map [:method 'it :description] [:raw "refs"]])
; => "refs.map(|it| it.description())"

(majutsu-tpl [:method
              [:map [:raw "self.parents()"] p [:raw "p.commit_id()"]]
              :join [:str "@comma{} "]])
; => "self.parents().map(|p| p.commit_id()).join(\"@comma{} \")"

(majutsu-tpl [:method [:raw "self.parents()"]
                      :map [:lambda [p] [:raw "p.commit_id()"]]
                      :join [:str "@comma{} "]])
; => "self.parents().map(|p| p.commit_id()).join(\"@comma{} \")"
@end lisp

All of these compile to jj's @samp{collection.map(|x| body)}.  Prefer direct
lambdas with @samp{:map} and @samp{:join} in new code; the binder form@comma{} @samp{:-map}@comma{} and
the @samp{:--map} family are shorthands for the same thing.

A reusable lambda can be defined as a helper that returns one:
@lisp
(majutsu-template-defun description-fn ()
  (:returns Lambda)
  [:lambda [c] [:description]])

(majutsu-tpl [:description-fn])
; => "|c| c.description()"

(majutsu-tpl [:method [:raw "refs"] :map [:description-fn]])
; => "refs.map(|c| c.description())"

(majutsu-template-defun description-with-suffix ((suffix Template))
  (:returns Lambda)
  `[:lambda [c] [:concat [:description] @comma{}suffix]])

(majutsu-tpl [:method [:raw "refs"] :map [:description-with-suffix [:str "!"]]])
; => "refs.map(|c| concat(c.description()@comma{} \"!\"))"
@end lisp

@node Extending the DSL
@section Extending the DSL

Use @samp{majutsu-template-defun} to define reusable template functions:
@lisp
(majutsu-template-defun my-helper ((label Template) (value Template :optional t))
  (:returns Template)
  `[:concat @comma{}label [:str ": "] @comma{}(or value [:str ""])])

(majutsu-tpl [:my-helper [:str "ID"] [:str "VAL"]])
; => "concat(\"ID\"@comma{} \": \"@comma{} \"VAL\")"
@end lisp

When a helper omits its body@comma{} @samp{majutsu-template-defun} defaults to a simple
wrapper around the same jj callable name:
@lisp
(majutsu-template-defun my-fill ((width Integer) (content Template))
  (:returns Template))

(majutsu-tpl [:my-fill 8 "x"])
; => "my-fill(8@comma{} \"x\")"
@end lisp

Syntax-level sugar should be defined separately with
@samp{majutsu-template-defspecial}@comma{} which receives raw forms and lowers them to more
primitive template syntax:
@lisp
(majutsu-template-defspecial :wrap-angle (body)
  `[:concat [:str "<"] @comma{}body [:str ">"]])

(majutsu-tpl [:wrap-angle [:str "x"]])
; => "concat(\"<\"@comma{} \"x\"@comma{} \">\")"
@end lisp

Owner-bound methods can also be defined locally in the DSL@. A body-less
@samp{majutsu-template-defmethod} / @samp{majutsu-template-defkeyword} declaration just
registers metadata for a native/rendered method@comma{} but providing a body turns it
into a local owner-bound lowering:
@lisp
(majutsu-template-defkeyword canonical-log-id Commit
  (:returns Template)
  [:if [:or [:hidden]
             [:divergent]]
       [:commit_id :shortest 8]
     [:change_id :shortest 8]])

(majutsu-tpl [:canonical-log-id] 'Commit)
; => "if((self.hidden() || self.divergent())@comma{} self.commit_id().shortest(8)@comma{} self.change_id().shortest(8))"

(majutsu-tpl [:method [:raw "p" :Commit] :canonical-log-id])
; => "if((p.hidden() || p.divergent())@comma{} p.commit_id().shortest(8)@comma{} p.change_id().shortest(8))"
@end lisp

@node Types
@section Types

The DSL supports Jujutsu's type system: @samp{Any}@comma{} @samp{String}@comma{} @samp{Boolean}@comma{} @samp{Integer}@comma{}
@samp{Template}@comma{} @samp{Commit}@comma{} @samp{Signature}@comma{} @samp{Timestamp}@comma{} @samp{List}@comma{} @samp{Option}@comma{} and more.
Type annotations can be added to @samp{:raw} nodes:
@lisp
[:raw "self" :Commit]  ; Declares the raw value has type Commit
@end lisp

Without an annotation a @samp{:raw} node is @samp{Unknown}: it compiles@comma{} and methods
called on it are not checked.

@samp{Any} stands for any expression; @samp{Template} is narrower and means something
that can be printed or concatenated.

Result types follow jj's: @samp{parents()} is a list of commits@comma{} @samp{lines()} a list
of strings@comma{} a mapped list is written @samp{(:list String)}@comma{} and @samp{first()} returns
the element type.

The checker is looser than jj in two places.  Where jj requires a string
literal@comma{} Majutsu accepts only an obvious literal@comma{} not an expression that
would become one.  jj's separate list-like result types are all plain lists
here.

@node Self Types by Command
@section Self Types by Command

The type of @samp{self} depends on the jj command the template is for:

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Command
@tab Self Type
@item log
@tab Commit
@item show
@tab Commit
@item evolog
@tab CommitEvolutionEntry
@item diff
@tab TreeDiffEntry
@item bookmark list
@tab CommitRef
@item tag list
@tab CommitRef
@item file annotate
@tab AnnotationLine
@item file list
@tab TreeEntry
@item file show
@tab TreeEntry
@item op log
@tab Operation
@item op show
@tab Operation
@item workspace list
@tab WorkspaceRef
@item config list
@tab Named keyword fields; @samp{value} is ConfigValue
@end multitable

Unlike the object-oriented rows above@comma{} @samp{jj config list} exposes named
keywords (@samp{name}@comma{} @samp{value}@comma{} @samp{overridden}@comma{} @samp{source}@comma{} and @samp{path}) rather than a
single @samp{ConfigValue} receiver.

